wildos-rdp (0.1.11) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The engine is a library Remote shares, with a whole-desktop session
    beside the app windows. It now links Qt Network, for the time a
    desktop tab takes to reach its computer.
  * App windows and desktops send the keyboard, mouse and wheel through
    the same code, so a fix to one reaches both.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Tue, 10 Nov 2026 07:45:00 +0000

wildos-rdp (0.1.10) trixie; urgency=medium

  * A loading window whose program ended before showing any window (it
    crashed, or gave up) says so: "X closed before it opened", with Try
    again and Close, instead of waiting as if it were still opening. The
    agent tells it, from what wild-guest sees in Windows.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 23:30:00 +0000

wildos-rdp (0.1.9) trixie; urgency=medium

  * --prepare-session signs in to Windows and leaves the session ready and
    disconnected, asking for no app, so the first app opened later takes
    over a session that is already there: about 3 seconds less, measured.
  * Every step of a connection is logged with its time (connected, app
    channel ready, Windows' windows sent, app asked for and started, first
    window), for working out where a slow open spends its time.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 23:00:00 +0000

wildos-rdp (0.1.8) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Programs with nothing to lose (Explorer, Character Map, Calculator,
    Settings and a few more) leave the screen the moment they are closed.
    Explorer redraws a folder window as it closes and can take seconds to
    end it; that read as a question, so the window stayed up meanwhile.
  * A window Windows disables after its close has a modal question up: it
    stays, or comes back if it was already hidden.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 22:00:00 +0000

wildos-rdp (0.1.7) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Only the connection's own loading window ignores the windows Windows
    sends first on connecting: an app opened in the session while the
    connection was starting can be among them, and its loading window
    takes it (it opened separately, with the generic W).
  * A window's program, once Windows names it, always replaces the generic
    W as its app id.
  * An app that opens before its loading window was due (board 9.12) gets
    its program's app id when the loading window did not know its app,
    not the generic W.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 21:30:00 +0000

wildos-rdp (0.1.6) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Where each app was last placed is saved again: the KWin script's
    numbers reached SavePlacement as doubles, which the method did not
    take, so no place was ever kept.
  * A window of a program Windows mode has no entry for, taken by a loading
    window that did not know its app, gets its program's own app id
    instead of keeping the generic W.
  * Opening a program Windows mode has no entry for shows the loading
    window too, named after the program.
  * Closing the loading window means no: the app's window, if Windows
    starts it anyway within two minutes, is closed as it comes instead of
    appearing after the person said no. Closing it while it is handing
    over to the app closes the app (it did nothing before).
  * A reconnection no longer opens a second copy of the connection's first
    app: FreeRDP asked Windows for it again every time it reconnected.
  * Windows that were already open when a connection began never take a
    loading window or end one: on connecting, Windows first sends every
    window already open (an app the last connection left), and one of
    those took the loading window of the app being opened, which then
    opened on its own. Windows marks the end of that first list.
  * A window closed while it was still taking over from its loading window
    ends that loading window; it waited again and took the next launch's
    window.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 20:50:00 +0000

wildos-rdp (0.1.5) trixie; urgency=medium

  * A loading window that takes its app's size (a fixed dialog, or an app
    that kept its own size) changes size about its centre: KWin moves and
    resizes it in one step. Without KWin it resizes from its corner as
    before.
  * A loading window that knows its program is never taken by a window
    whose program Windows has not named (a window another app left open,
    sent again on reconnecting); a window named too late to be taken makes
    the loading window give way.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 20:30:00 +0000

wildos-rdp (0.1.4) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The loading window hands over as soon as the app has drawn, whether or
    not the app takes the loading window's size. A window Windows will not
    resize (Character Map) gives the loading window its own size as it
    arrives, and is remembered at it for next time.
  * A window that draws nothing is shown after 4 seconds anyway; a window
    of the app's program the loading window did not take (a first-run
    question) makes it give way; a window owned by another program whose
    title names the app is taken. The slow state only ever means Windows
    itself is slow.
  * The light keeps running its laps in the slow state, so it never looks
    hung. Reduced motion still shows a still frame.
  * The fade from the loading window into the app is finished by the
    clock as well as by the compositor's frames, so a lost frame can no
    longer leave the app half faded in.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 19:30:00 +0000

wildos-rdp (0.1.3) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Closing without flicker: a closed window stays exactly as it is
    while Windows deals with it. Gone within half a second: it simply
    goes. Drawing again or opening a dialog: it is asking, and stays. No
    word from Windows at all: it leaves the screen and the taskbar after
    half a second, and is handled in the background as before.
  * Alt+F4 no longer leaves Windows' key tips or menu open behind it.
  * Popups Windows draws with transparency (rounded menus, key tips) keep
    it instead of showing black corners.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 18:30:00 +0000

wildos-rdp (0.1.2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Closing is ours to show at once: a window closed from the taskbar or
    with Alt+F4 leaves the screen and the taskbar in the same frame, and
    Windows is told in the background. A question it asks before closing
    (Save changes?) is shown straight away, and the window comes back if
    the answer is not to close; one that asks inside itself (Windows 11's
    Paint and Notepad) keeps drawing, and comes back for the answer. A
    window Windows does not close stays
    hidden: with unsaved changes it is kept and a notification offers to
    show it; with none, the agent ends its program after 10 seconds.
  * Windows of a dropped connection leave the screen after 2 seconds of
    trying to reconnect, and come back when it does.
  * A loading window closes when the app was already open and Windows
    only brought its window forward.
  * Leaving never waits for a FreeRDP call that does not return.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +0000

wildos-rdp (0.1.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The loading window goes to the first window of the app's own program,
    asked of Windows, not to whichever window comes first; a window that is
    hidden or cloaked before the app shows in it hands the loading window
    back, so none is ever left on the taskbar.
  * Windows' cloaked windows stay off screen.
  * Every window of an app's program takes the app's id; a window nothing
    is known about gets its program's own id and the icon Windows sends,
    never Windows mode's W.
  * One log line for every decision about a window, for testing on real
    hardware.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 17:30:00 +0000

